/ListMics.class
/ProbeMics.class
/scratch_test.kt
/fix_gif_crash.kt
*.old2
/build_baseline_test.log

# SECURITY: ad-hoc investigation scripts must never be committed.
#
# `scratch/` was added in 17e1584 ("ui improv") and contained eleven Python
# scripts. Seven of them hardcoded a live matrix.org access token in an
# `Authorization: Bearer` header, plus the account's MXID, device id, ed25519
# identity key, curve25519 identity key and a live one-time key. A bearer token
# in a git repository is permanent: deleting the files in a later commit does not
# remove it from any clone that already pulled, and no rotation happens
# automatically. History has to be rewritten, and the token revoked at the
# homeserver -- both of which are the credential owner's call, not a code fix.
#
# These scripts are useful work, so keep them locally; do not publish them. If
# one is genuinely needed by someone else, redact the token and the key material
# first, not after.
#
# Rules here:
#   /scratch/            the directory
#   /scratch_*/          the older naming convention
#   *.py                 one-off scripts at the root (the tree has no real Python)
#   .env / *.local       never commit a local secret file, whatever it is called
#
# A pattern without a reason reads as cargo cult, so: there is no Python in the
# product, `build.gradle.kts` never invokes a .py, and the only ones that ever
# existed were debugging aids. `scratch_test.kt` above is listed separately
# because it is Kotlin.
/scratch/
/scratch_*/
*.py
.env
*.local

# CMake / Visual Studio build caches.
#
# `**/build/` above does not reach these, because the generated directories are
# not always named "build" -- webrtc-jni uses build2, and orbit_audio_jni keeps a
# parallel out/ tree. 166 of these were tracked, each carrying absolute paths from
# whichever machine generated it, so every clone disagreed with the committed
# cache.
#
# The previous version of this comment claimed "They are now untracked; this stops
# them coming back" while listing no rule that reached build2/. So
# webrtc-jni/src/main/cpp/build2/ came straight back, 368 files of .obj, .tlog,
# .lib, .exp and an absolute-path CMakeCache.txt. The rules below are the ones
# that actually match that tree; keep this list and the claim in step.
#
# The directory names first, because the extension globs below do not catch
# generated *files* like .obj/.tlog/.exp.
**/build2/
**/out/
**/CMakeCache.txt
**/CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CMakeUserPresets.json
LastTest.log
LastTestsFailed.log
Testing/
*.sln
*.vcxproj
*.vcxproj.filters
VSInheritEnvironments.txt
TargetDirectories.txt
VCTargetsPath.txt
compile_commands.json

# The compiler/linker outputs a CMake configure drops next to its cache. These are
# exactly the files that carried another machine's absolute paths.
#
# NOTE: deliberately no blanket `*.dll`, `*.lib` or `*.pdb` rule here. `*.dll` is
# already handled above (with the app-resources carve-outs), the WebRTC decoders and
# the LiveKit plugin are committed binaries that MUST stay tracked, and webrtc-jni
# links against committed `.lib` files. A glob that quietly untracks shipped media
# is a far worse mistake than a few stale `.tlog` files, which the directory rules
# above already catch.
**/CMakeFiles/
**/*.tlog/
**/*.idb.tlog/
*.obj
*.tlog
*.ipdb
*.ilk
*.iobj
*.exp
*.recipe
*.stamp
*.ninja_deps
*.ninja_log
generate.stamp.list

# Stale editor/merge leftovers of a source file. *.old2 was already listed;
# these are the other names the same mistake turns up under.
*.old
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*~

# Local SQLite databases
memory.db*
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Cargo / Rust build caches
target/
**/target/
